# A Transformer-Based Framework for Predicting CRISPR DNA Repair Outcomes

## PhD Thesis (Revised Version)

**Date:** February 2026

---

## Abstract

The CRISPR-Cas9 system has revolutionized genome editing, but the unpredictability of DNA repair outcomes after double-strand breaks remains a critical barrier to therapeutic applications. Existing computational models, such as inDelphi and FORECasT, predict indel distributions with reasonable accuracy within limited cell types but fail to generalize across different genomic contexts and experimental conditions. This thesis proposes a novel transformer-based framework, **X-Repair**, that predicts CRISPR DNA repair outcomes through a mathematically rigorous architecture combining uniform multi-scale tokenization, rotary positional embeddings centered at the cut site, and explicit microhomology-aware attention mechanisms. The model achieves superior generalization through adaptive cell-type conditioning via AdaLN, strand-symmetry constraints, and a composite loss function incorporating distribution smoothness and calibration objectives. We demonstrate that X-Repair outperforms existing methods on cross-cell-type generalization while maintaining interpretability through attention visualization and requiring only a single consumer GPU for training. This work provides a theoretically grounded, biologically informed, and practically deployable solution to CRISPR outcome prediction.

