## Chapter 3: Computational Background

### 3.1 Sequence Modeling

Biological sequences (DNA, RNA, protein) can be represented as strings over a finite alphabet. Early computational approaches used position weight matrices (PWMs), k-mer frequencies, or handcrafted features. Machine learning models such as logistic regression, support vector machines, and random forests have been applied to various genomics tasks [16]. More recently, deep learning has enabled models to learn hierarchical representations directly from sequence data without manual feature engineering.

The shift to learned representations has been particularly impactful in genomics, where the space of potentially relevant features (k-mers, motifs, secondary structures) is combinatorially large and context-dependent. Convolutional neural networks dominated early deep learning approaches to sequence modeling, leveraging weight sharing to detect motifs at arbitrary positions. However, CNNs have limited receptive fields and struggle with long-range dependencies.

### 3.2 Transformers and Attention

The transformer architecture [17] has become the dominant approach for sequence modeling in natural language processing and has been adapted to biological sequences [18, 19]. Its key component is the attention mechanism, which computes a weighted sum of input representations, allowing the model to focus on relevant parts of the sequence regardless of distance.

For a sequence of tokens with embeddings $X \in \mathbb{R}^{L \times d}$, self-attention computes:

$$
\text{Attention}(Q, K, V) = \text{softmax}\left(\frac{QK^T}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right) V
$$

where $Q = XW_Q$, $K = XW_K$, $V = XW_V$ are linear projections. The scaling factor $\sqrt{d_k}$ prevents the dot products from growing too large, which would cause the softmax to saturate. Multi-head attention runs $h$ such operations in parallel with different learned projections and concatenates the results:

$$
\text{MHA}(X) = \text{Concat}(\text{head}_1, \dots, \text{head}_h) W_O
$$

This allows the model to attend to different aspects of the input simultaneously—for instance, one head might focus on local context while another captures long-range dependencies.

**Positional information** is crucial for transformers, as the attention mechanism itself is permutation-invariant. Various encoding schemes have been proposed:

- **Absolute positional embeddings** (original Transformer [17]): Sinusoidal functions or learned vectors added to input embeddings
- **Relative positional encodings** (Shaw et al., 2018): Modify attention scores based on pairwise distances
- **Rotary position embeddings (RoPE)** (Su et al., 2021 [23]): Apply rotation to query/key vectors, encoding relative position directly in the inner product

For CRISPR repair prediction, relative position with respect to the cut site is critical—a microhomology pattern 10bp upstream has different implications than the same pattern 50bp upstream. This motivates our choice of RoPE with cut-site-centered coordinates (Section 6.2).

